> If someone tries a portscan on our Tru64 systems (Compaq Tru64
> UNIX V5.1, Rev. 732), some daemons die (rpc.lockd, rpc.yppasswdd,
> rpc.statd).
> This probem can be repeated by a simple 'nmap -sT -v hostname',
> and I have the vague feeling, that this is not the desired reaction
> to a portscan.
>
> Is there a solution for this problem?
>
> thank you in advance,
> Erik

One way of reducing the affects of the portscan is to change the setting the
value of a parameter, sominconn, to 65535. This will make Tru64 UNIX more
hardened against SYN attacks. This change can be made using the following
command:
# /sbin/sysconfig -r socket sominconn=65535
# /sbin/sysconfig -r socket somaxconn-65535

Also search www.cert.org for references to nmap and tru64.  There are a
number of new security patches for Tru64 that specifically refer to nmap and
portscanning killing NIS daemons - eg: SSRTM541
(http://ftp.support.compaq.com/patches/public/unix/v5.1/t64v51b18-c0102003-1
4238-es-20020507.README)




