Appended below my signature is the start of a Perl script which will
convert the /etc/passwd.dir and /etc/passwd.pag files into a not quite
complete /etc/passwd.txt.  You're going to need to either work on the
script to complete it (It was really written for .htpasswd files with
less information), or you'll need to go through the resulting passwd.txt
file and fix up userids, groupids, and take ':' characters out of the
GCOS field.  Whether you want to add to the script or fix up the output
manually probably depends on how many users you have...

When you're done, rename /etc/passwd.txt to /etc/passwd.

I'm sorry I don't have a more complete solution, but I hope this at
least helps get you started in the right direction.

Reloading from backup *would* be a *lot* easier, of course...  ;-)

-- 
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Sylvain Robitaille                              syl@alcor.concordia.ca
 
Systems analyst                                   Concordia University
Instructional & Information Technology        Montreal, Quebec, Canada
----------------------------------------------------------------------

#!/local/paths/perl -wT
#
# file: dbm2txt.pl
#
# 2000/08/30 Sylvain Robitaille: Quick hack to extract text password
#                                file from DBM file. 

my ($key, $value);
my ($db_file) = @ARGV;

if(!defined $db_file) {
  die "Usage:\n $0 <filename>\n";
}

# Untaint input:
if( $db_file =~ m%^([^`\\<>|]+)$% ) {
  $db_file = $1;
} else {
  die "$db_file tainted\n";
}

dbmopen  %PW, "$db_file", 0666 or die "can't dbmopen $db_file: $!\n";
open  TXTPW, "> $db_file.txt" or die "can't open $db_file.txt: $!\n";

# Make sure we'll be writing to the output file:
my $oldfh = select TXTPW;

# translate the input:
while (($key,$value) = each %PW) {
  $value =~ s/[\0\s]+/:/g;
  $value =~ s/:$//g;
  print "$value\n";
}

# Reset our stdout:
select $oldfh;

close TXTPW;
dbmclose %PW;



