In article <3d3d33a1$1@usenetgateway.com>, d_diddy <member@dbforums.com> writes:

>I am having the same error message as the original message posted,
>however for a DECHZ50AX....

For the HSZ and HSG subsystems, the event data written to host's
event log for back-end device errors includes device locater
bytes to identify the back-end port, target and LUN.  If uerf(8)
will run on your Tru64 UNIX system, it should be able to format
the data, though DECevent will do a better job.  uerf(8) may not
work on any V5 version, so you'll have to DECevent.  It is on the
Associated Product CDROM for most versions.

For V4 and earlier versions, the device name encodes the bus, target
and LUN of the logical unit presented by the HSZ.  With access to
the console of the HSZ you can track this back to the back-end
device.  On V5, you'll need to use hwmgr to get the bus, target
and LUN of the presented unit.

>
>Is there any way of locating where on a rack this disk is (for example
>by flashing an LED etc.) so that I can "give it a good shake"?!

Once you identify the disk by name or PTL (port-target-lun), you can
use the HSZ LOCATE command to blink one of the lights.

If the message got was precisely the one in the original message,
you'll need to take care that the host only sees the presented
logical units of the HSZ family controllers.  Those units may be
simple disks (JBOD), RAID-5, Mirrors, Stripes or Striped Mirror
sets.  The unit that failed to start was the logical unit presented
by the controller.  The back-end could be any number of disks or
no disk at all depending on what caused the unit to fail.

