I responded to the poster directly via e-mail, but on the off chance
anyone else is interested in my hacked-together script:

#!/usr/bin/perl

use NDBM_File;

tie(%PW, NDBM_File, "/etc/passwd", O_RDONLY, 0) ||
    die "Can't tie /etc/passwd: $!\n";

foreach $id (keys %PW) {
    $data = $PW{$id};
    $pos = 0;
    while (($c = substr($data, $pos++, 1)) ne "\0") {
	print $c;
    }
    print ":";
    while (($c = substr($data, $pos++, 1)) ne "\0") {
	print $c;
    }
    print ":";
    @nums = unpack("i i i", substr($data, $pos, 12));
    print "$nums[0]:$nums[1]:";
    $pos += 13;
    while (($c = substr($data, $pos++, 1)) ne "\0") {
	print $c;
    }
    print ":";
    while (($c = substr($data, $pos++, 1)) ne "\0") {
	print $c;
    }
    print ":";
    while (($c = substr($data, $pos++, 1)) ne "\0") {
	print $c;
    }
    print "\n";
}
untie(%PW);

Notes: The data values stored in the /etc/passwd.{dir,pag} 
hash are a mushed-together binary format resistent to elegant
solutions using unpack() or split(). Each password file line
has two entries in the hash: one for login keys, one for uid
keys; so this code outputs each user twice.
I suggested that the output be piped through 'sort -u'.

I tested this as much as possible on a Tru64 system... although
I didn't go as far as to blow away /etc/passwd.


