Dave Uhring <dmuhring@yahoo.com> writes:
> That is really not very helpful at all.  I am just beginning to get used 
> to Tru64 Unix and I have found it to be quite different from other 
> Unices and BSDs, not to mention Linux.

Unfortunately, just about every Unix has its own ideas about
organizing the boot process.  While Tru64 userland is mostly
BSDish, the boot process looks more SysVish.

> Solaris, for instance, has a totally different setup for network 
> configuration.  Tru64's is more similar to FreeBSD.

Thank goodness it isn't like Solaris--Solaris network configuration is
a twisty maze of mostly undocumented files (e.g. /etc/notrouter, or
the mess that decides how router discovery is done).  Tru64 at least
has some semblance of organization.

> How do you expect a new user to figure out where the machine's IP 
> address is to be set without knowing the name of the file where it is 
> set?

HPQ expects you to use the netconfig command.  The netconfig man page
does tell you about rc.config, and the rc.config man page tells you
about rcmgr.

However, I've found that with just about every Unix system, at some
point you have to follow the boot process, figure out where the boot
scripts are, and read a few of them.  With Tru64, once you find
/sbin/init.d the rest is pretty simple--the boot scripts are
straightforward, and having most configuration in /etc/rc.config
simplifies figuring out a system's configuration.

> rcmgr(8) just does not exist in any BSD, Solaris or Linux.

Some other systems do have something similar--/etc/rc.conf on NetBSD
and chkconfig on IRIX, for example--but the details all are always
different.

-dan


