In article <uisim48f9rqc27@corp.supernews.com>,
Dave Uhring  <dmuhring@yahoo.com> wrote:
> Solaris, for instance, has a totally different setup for network 
> configuration.  Tru64's is more similar to FreeBSD.

That's not surprising. Tru64 is based on 4.3-Reno, the last 4.3 release.
FreeBSD is based on 4.4.

In fact you can get quite a long way by treating Tru64 as FreeBSD with
a System V inittab and rc*.d directories. The biggest difference you're
likely to run into with any great frequency is AdvFS.

I'm getting into quite a love-hate relationship with AdvFS. :)

> How do you expect a new user to figure out where the machine's IP 
> address is to be set without knowing the name of the file where it is 
> set?  rcmgr(8) just does not exist in any BSD, Solaris or Linux.

All rcmgr does is safely edit /etc/rc.config, which is very similar to
/etc/rc.conf on FreeBSD. 

Let's say you have a machine with two interfaces to /24 subnets, with
an Intel EtherExpress card and a DEC de500 and addresses 10.0.0.43 and
10.1.0.43 respectively.

In FreeBSD you set the IP address like this:

/etc/rc.conf:
network_interfaces="fxp0 lo0 dc0"
ifconfig_lo0="inet 127.0.0.1"
ifconfig_fxp0="inet 10.0.0.43 media autoselect netmask 255.255.255.0"
ifconfig_dc0="inet 10.1.0.43 media autoselect netmask 255.255.255.0"

In Tru64 you do this:

/etc/rc.config:
NUM_NETCONFIG="2"
NETDEV_0="ee0"
NETDEV_1="tu0"
IFCONFIG_0="10.0.0.43 netmask 255.255.255.0"
IFCONFIG_1="10.1.0.43 netmask 255.255.255.0"

It should be pretty obvious what else goes in there.

