Hi folks.  There have been people asking in the past about whether
or not IPFilter works on Tru64 to which I can now say it does!
Well, the latest alpha does, anyway :)

The source code can be found at:
http://coombs.anu.edu.au/~avalon/ipf40a23.tar.gz
Hope page for ipfilter:
http://coombs.anu.edu.au/~avalon/ip-filter.html

You will need a working compiler to get going, I've not tested with
gcc, but if it produces code as good as the Tru64 cc, you should be
fine.

This is the first time IPFilter has run on Tru64 and given this, there
are some niggles - well, at least one: /etc/services on Tru64 5.1 does
not list port 123 as being for ntp and so regression test i12 will fail
if you run them (make -k to zoom past).

There is no chance in hell for any support of a prior version of Tru64,
the API IPFilter uses does not appear any earlier than 5.1.

I've only got single-CPU alpha boxes so I'm not 100% sure if the locking
is safe under Tru64 (seems to work fine on multi-cpu Sun SPARC boxes.)

If you're worried about it being "alpha", what I can say is this:

The regression tests are there to show that the filtering works as it
is expected, so security should not be an issue.  Whilst it should be
moderately stable, the reason it is still "alpha" is I want to do more
work on adding "features", yet.

Darren


