You can use userdel to delete Enhanced Security users,
if the OS version you are running supports it.  I forget
exactly when the functionality was added, but older versions
of dxaccounts and userdel will only allow you to retire
a user, not delete the user.  It is recommended that you
retire Enhanced Security users instead of deleting them 
because of the security risks associated with deleting a 
user and re-using the uid.  The new user could have 
unintended access left over from the old user. 

If you want to go ahead and delete the user in spite of
the security risks, "man userdel" will show if your version 
supports deleting Enhanced Security users (the -D option).

If your version's userdel doesn't support this, you can 
still delete the user using "edauth -r" (see "man edauth")
and deleting the user by hand.

Ann


