"Choi, WooYoung" <yupikr@yahoo.com> wrote in
news:ag42o4$crt$1@news1.kornet.net: 
> Hi, everyone..
> 
> The system is Tru64 Unix v4.0f  (BL17), 8ea 6/700 Mhz CPU on A8400,
> and one of Two Node cluster v1.6
> 
> kernel process consuming cpu time 200% ~ 400% for several hours.
> I am checking using "ps -eflm" command and I found that bss_rm threads
> spend cpu time (almost 100% systime).
> 
> what is threads "bss_rm" ?

It is the block shipping server for reflective memory a.k.a memory channel.  
I can think of only a handful of people walking the earth that would know 
the answer to that question, and I'm one of them :-).

> what can I check for this problem ?

The fact it's running a lot means the other node in your cluster is 
requesting a lot of disk blocks from disks that are being served by this 
node.  There's a lot of things that can be done to improve this 
performance, things like giving the client node direct access to the disk, 
improving memory channel performance by putting it into a different slot in 
the 8400, etc.  I think the cluster document set does a pretty good job of 
going through the options.
 
> Thanks you..

No problem.  


