As always, YMMV, but here are my results for STREAMS benchmarks
an XP1000 (666mhz EV6) running Tru64 v5.1a 
better speedup would probably be obtained with -arch=ev6 or whever the correct
syntax is.
runt: cc -fast
-------------------------------------------------------------
Function      Rate (MB/s)   RMS time     Min time     Max time
Copy:         780.1859       0.0272       0.0205       0.0352
Scale:        744.7066       0.0268       0.0215       0.0293
Add:          768.0000       0.0400       0.0312       0.0430
Triad:        767.9736       0.0414       0.0313       0.0469


runt: gcc (v3.0.3) -O3 -DU_ALPHA
-------------------------------------------------------------
Function      Rate (MB/s)   RMS time     Min time     Max time
Copy:         399.6014       0.0587       0.0400       0.0898
Scale:        381.0157       0.0602       0.0420       0.0986
Add:          431.1505       0.1496       0.0557       0.4150
Triad:        372.3588       0.0711       0.0645       0.0781

as you can see, cc is much faster for streams, if your app is memory
bound, this will be a great help.
My particular app is a C prog that spends ~50% of its time doing
1/sqrt(), and the majority of the other 50% doing other misc floating
point operations. for this gcc and cc are approx equal. (I lost my
benchmarks to a failed disk.) This is a bit of
a suprise, but I don't think that cc is as well optimized for the ev6 as
it was for ev5, based on the lack of work compaq seems to have put into
everything they inherited from DEC. gcc-2.9x was blown away by cc, but
3.0 seems to have closed the gap quite a bit. Make sure to use the
CXML/DXML libraries when you compile.


